---
import ParameterModulator from '../../components/demos/ParameterModulator.tsx';
import Frame from '../../components/diagrams/Frame.astro';
import ParameterDiagram from '../../components/diagrams/ParameterDiagram.astro';
import Snippet from '../../components/Snippet.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';

const get = `const intensity = engine.parameter('intensity', 0.3);
intensity.set(0.7);                       // updates all bound targets`;

const bind = `intensity.bindTo((v) => engine.bus('music').level = v, {
  from: 0.4, to: 1.0, curve: 'easeInOut',
});

intensity.bindTo((v) => filter.setFrequency(v), {
  from: 800, to: 12000, curve: 'easeIn',
});`;

const subscribe = `const off = intensity.subscribe((v) => console.log('intensity:', v));
// later: off();`;
---
<Docs
  title="Parameter"
  description="A named float you drive at runtime. Subscribers update immediately. Bind it to bus levels, FX values, anything."
  eyebrow="Concept"
>
  <p>
    Tension rises. You want the music bed up, the ambience down, the low-pass
    on the drone opening, and the reverb wet going with it. Written by hand
    that is four setters that have to stay in step, plus a fifth the day
    someone adds another layer.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <a href="/api/Parameter/"><code>Parameter</code></a> is a named float you
    set at runtime, and a set of bindings that map it onto other values. You
    set the parameter; everything bound to it updates through its own range
    and curve. It is the seam between an idea a designer has and the ten
    numbers that idea turns into.
  </p>

  <h2>Mental model</h2>
  <Frame caption="One parameter feeds many bound targets. Each binding has its own range + curve.">
    <ParameterDiagram />
  </Frame>

  <h2>API surface</h2>
  <Snippet code={`engine.parameter(name, initial?): Parameter

class Parameter {
  readonly name: string;
  readonly value: number;
  set(v: number): void;
  subscribe(fn: (v: number) => void): () => void;
  bindTo(setter: (mapped: number) => void, opts?: {
    from?: number;        // default 0
    to?:   number;        // default 1
    curve?: 'linear' | 'easeIn' | 'easeOut' | 'easeInOut' | 'equal-power';
  }): () => void;
}`} lang="ts" title="Parameter" />

  <h2>Live demo</h2>
  <p>One slider driving a bus level and a derived value, each through its own range and curve.</p>
  <ParameterModulator client:visible />

  <h2>Recipes</h2>

  <h3>Get / set</h3>
  <Snippet code={get} lang="ts" />

  <h3>Bind to one or more targets</h3>
  <Snippet code={bind} lang="ts" />

  <h3>Subscribe for UI updates</h3>
  <Snippet code={subscribe} lang="ts" />

  <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
  <div class="pitfall">
    <div class="pitfall-title">Parameters aren't ramps.</div>
    A parameter is a discrete value. Subscribers turn it into a ramp on their
    own, usually by writing to a bus level, which has its own 10 ms ramp
    inside. When you need a guaranteed N-millisecond crossfade, use a
    <a href="/concepts/snapshot/">Snapshot</a>.
  </div>
  <div class="pitfall">
    <div class="pitfall-title">Don't <code>set</code> in a tight loop.</div>
    Each set runs every subscriber synchronously. Driving a parameter from
    <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> means 60 Hz, which is fine. Anything
    denser than that, throttle it.
  </div>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/concepts/snapshot/">Snapshot</a> handles discrete mix states, crossfaded.</li>
    <li><a href="/concepts/bus/">Bus</a> is the most common bind target.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
